@startuml
title Region solve - full pipeline (current, plain-language)

start

partition "before ANY world gen (build time, once)" {
  :for every possible group of up to 8 biomes,\ncheck at least one biome in the list is legal\nnext to all of them at the same time;
  if (any group has NO such biome?) then (yes)
    :CRASH the build on purpose -\nthe biome list itself has a real gap,\nfix it before shipping;
    end
  else (no)
  endif
  note right
    this is what makes the final "CRASH on purpose" box
    far below PROVEN unreachable, not just rare - a real
    production bug (badlands + ocean shared no third
    common neighbor) was found and fixed this way.
  end note
}

:place seed points across the region\n(SeedGrid, +margin so edge triangles have real neighbors);
:connect seed points into triangles\n(Delaunay triangulation);
:work out which triangles touch which\n(TriangleAdjacency, max 3 real neighbors each);

partition "pick each triangle's biome (CSP)" {
  :any triangle whose center already falls inside\na neighboring region's solved area gets FIXED\nto that neighbor's own decision;
  :work out a sensible max triangle count per biome\n(how many of ITS OWN patches could fit in ITS\nfair share of this region's area - same number\nfor every biome once you're fair by area);
  :for every other (free) triangle, pick a biome that is\nallowed next to all its already-decided neighbors;
  note right
    picks are ALSO fairness-biased: each biome has a
    target share of triangles (based on its size range,
    squared, since a patch is an area not a line) - an
    under-represented biome is preferred whenever two
    choices are equally legal, UNLESS that biome already
    hit its max triangle count for this region (then it
    sorts last, but can still be picked if truly the only
    legal option - never blocks a solution from existing).
  end note
  :backtrack if a choice leads to a dead end;
}

:give each triangle a random starting radius\n(from its biome's declared width range);

partition "correctRadii - fix radius, generation time only\n(runs up to 3 passes, one triangle at a time)" {
  :FIRST, do one full test-render of the region\nand add up how many cells each BIOME won;
  :compare that to each biome's fair share of the\nwhole region (same size-based quota as the CSP step);
  :mark a biome "starved" if its TOTAL area (all its\ntriangles combined) is under half its fair share;

  while (still passes left AND something changed last pass?) is (yes)
    :test-render again, count this triangle's own cells;
    if (won zero cells AND its biome is starved?) then (yes)
      :allow a MUCH bigger radius ceiling\nand grow faster;
      note right
        ONLY a triangle that is currently
        winning nothing gets this boost - a
        triangle already contributing real
        area is left alone even if its biome
        is starved overall, so this can't
        re-inflate an already-fine patch.
      end note
    else (no)
      :normal radius ceiling\n(1.5x its own biome's max width);
    endif
    if (won zero cells?) then (yes)
      :grow radius by the growth step, capped at the ceiling;
    else (no)
      if (measured size outside its own target range?) then (yes)
        :nudge radius toward target -\nbut the STEP ITSELF is also capped\n(at most ~2x per pass, either way),\nnot just the final ceiling;
        note right
          without this step cap, one pass could
          slam a radius almost straight to its
          ceiling when measured size was tiny -
          with several biomes doing that at once,
          the whole region's radii would oscillate
          forever instead of settling down.
        end note
      else (no)
        :leave this triangle's radius alone;
      endif
    endif
  endwhile (no more passes or nothing changed)
}

partition "final render - zero illegal touches, guaranteed" {
  :score every cell against every nearby triangle\n(closer + bigger radius wins);
  :grow the map out cell-by-cell,\nmost-confident cell first;
  if (best biome for this cell is legal against\nevery already-decided neighbor around it\n(all 8 directions)?) then (yes)
    :commit it;
  else (no)
    if (any OTHER biome in the graph is legal against\nall of this cell's decided neighbors?) then (yes)
      :commit that one instead (fallback scan);
    else (no)
      :CRASH on purpose - the build-time check above\nalready proves this can't happen for a\nwell-formed biome list, so reaching this\nmeans that proof was skipped or the list\nchanged since;
      end
    endif
  endif
}

:done - one biome per cell, zero illegal adjacencies;

stop

@enduml
